1
00:00:01,460 --> 00:00:04,600
死亡终有一天降临在我们每个人身上。
Death will someday come for us all.

2
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:09,990
但逝去的人真的代表永远离开我们了么？
But are the dead really gone forever?

3
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:14,130
药物的发展与计算机科学的飞跃进步
Advances in medicine and bold leaps in computer science

4
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:18,130
也许很快就能让死去的人重新行走在地面上...
may soon allow the dead to walk the earth again...

5
00:00:18,920 --> 00:00:22,880
还是以别的新奇的形式存在。
or survive in some other strange, new form.

6
00:00:24,260 --> 00:00:27,500
我们能否使死者复活？
Can we resurrect the dead?

7
00:00:31,560 --> 00:00:36,560
《同摩根·弗里曼一起穿越虫洞》
空间、时间、生命本身
Space, time, life itself.

8
00:00:38,460 --> 00:00:43,430
宇宙的秘密，尽在穿越虫洞
The secrets of the cosmos lie through the wormhole.

9
00:00:48,510 --> 00:00:53,570
我们能否使死者复活？

10
00:00:55,510 --> 00:00:59,570
死亡是我们的人生的终点站，
Death is our ultimate destination,

11
00:00:59,690 --> 00:01:03,580
一个从来没有人能返回的地方，
a place from which no one ever returns.

12
00:01:03,690 --> 00:01:06,780
但如果死亡不是结束呢？
But what if death was not the end?

13
00:01:06,890 --> 00:01:09,280
我们每个人都有一张基因组蓝图，
We each have a genetic blueprint,

14
00:01:09,390 --> 00:01:11,580
现代科学已经能够阅读它了，
one that science can now read.

15
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:16,860
很快，也许就能在我死后让我的身体重生，
Soon, it may be possible to reproduce my body after I die.

16
00:01:16,970 --> 00:01:20,060
但储存在这里的一生的
But what about the lifetime of knowledge and experience

17
00:01:20,170 --> 00:01:22,160
知识和经历呢？
contained in here?

18
00:01:22,270 --> 00:01:24,060
我们是否会有挽回
Will we ever have the tools

19
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:27,940
死者身体和灵魂的工具呢？
to raise body and soul from the dead?

20
00:01:32,580 --> 00:01:37,580
我们所有人最后都会与死亡面对面，
All of us must eventually come face-to-face with death.

21
00:01:37,690 --> 00:01:41,450
不论我们多么希望握紧住自己所爱的人，
No matter how hard we wish to hold on to someone we love,

22
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:46,550
但有时，我们却不得不放手。
sometimes, we just have to let go.

23
00:01:46,670 --> 00:01:50,430
我曾经养了一只狗，经常和他一起去探险，
I had a dog I loved to go exploring with.

24
00:01:54,040 --> 00:01:57,140
但是虫子吃掉了它的心脏，
But worms had eaten away at his heart.

25
00:02:06,420 --> 00:02:10,980
永别的痛苦深深地刻在心里，
The pain of saying goodbye forever cuts deep,

26
00:02:11,090 --> 00:02:13,420
可这又无法避免，
but it is unavoidable.

27
00:02:17,700 --> 00:02:20,060
还是可以避免？
Or is it?

28
00:02:31,940 --> 00:02:34,770
这位病人已经死了，
This patient is dead.

29
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:37,900
他没有心跳，没有呼吸，
He has no heartbeat, no respiration,

30
00:02:38,020 --> 00:02:41,410
没有血液流过他的心脏，
no blood flowing through his heart.

31
00:02:41,520 --> 00:02:47,110
但今天，他会被这个人复活，
But today, he will be brought back to life by this man,

32
00:02:47,230 --> 00:02:52,750
耶鲁纽黑文医院的心脏外科医生John Elefteriades
heart surgeon John Elefteriades of Yale-New Haven hospital.

33
00:02:52,860 --> 00:02:58,590
John经常杀死他的病人，然后再复活他们。
John regularly kills his patients, then resurrects them.

34
00:02:58,700 --> 00:03:03,730
我曾在医学院进行医师培训，
Once I got to medical school and residency,

35
00:03:03,840 --> 00:03:06,270
心脏手术是我唯一想做的。
heart surgery was the only thing I ever wanted to do.

36
00:03:06,380 --> 00:03:10,440
那个时候，我感觉自己就像使用药物的
At that time, it was kind of like being a fighter pilot

37
00:03:10,550 --> 00:03:12,400
战斗机飞行员，你知道我的意思。
within medicine, if you know what I mean.

38
00:03:12,520 --> 00:03:15,640
这是在探索前沿，风险很高。
It was exploring the frontiers. It was high-risk.

39
00:03:15,750 --> 00:03:20,990
John现在仍飞行于外科手术的地平线边缘。
John still flies at the edge of the surgical horizon.

40
00:03:22,490 --> 00:03:26,550
今天，他要修复一个严重受损的心脏，
Today, he's trying to repair a severely damaged heart,

41
00:03:26,670 --> 00:03:31,670
但修复过程中不能有血液流过，
a heart that can't be fixed while blood flows through it.

42
00:03:34,340 --> 00:03:38,000
这样就必须把心脏关闭，但如果这么做
The heart must be shut down, but doing so

43
00:03:38,110 --> 00:03:40,800
就会切断病人脑部的血液供应，
will cut off the blood supply to the patient's brain,

44
00:03:40,910 --> 00:03:43,280
而导致脑部缺氧。
starving it of oxygen.

45
00:03:45,950 --> 00:03:50,180
John有45分钟的时间进行手术。
John needs 45 minutes to operate.

46
00:03:50,290 --> 00:03:51,710
在正常温度下，
At normal temperatures,

47
00:03:51,820 --> 00:03:54,890
大脑会在缺血5分钟后死亡。
the brain will begin to die after just five minutes.

48
00:03:57,390 --> 00:03:59,590
而John激进的办法就是
John's radical solution

49
00:03:59,700 --> 00:04:04,030
把病人体温降低，进入假死状态。
is to freeze the patient into a state of suspended animation.

50
00:04:06,340 --> 00:04:08,330
脑无法承受
The brain doesn't tolerate

51
00:04:08,440 --> 00:04:12,060
瞬间血流中断，
more than a momentary interruption of blood flow.

52
00:04:12,180 --> 00:04:14,030
如果血流中断
If the interruption of blood flow

53
00:04:14,150 --> 00:04:15,600
超过几分钟，
goes beyond several minutes,

54
00:04:15,710 --> 00:04:17,600
脑细胞就开始死亡，
then the brain cells start to die,

55
00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:21,410
这也是我们使用低温的手段
and that's where the protection of low temperature

56
00:04:21,520 --> 00:04:24,080
能够给我们机会保护
gives us the opportunity to protect

57
00:04:24,190 --> 00:04:25,940
非常脆弱的脑。
the very vulnerable brain.

58
00:04:26,060 --> 00:04:30,320
从病人体内抽出温热的血液，
The patient's warm blood has been drained from his body,

59
00:04:30,430 --> 00:04:33,590
流过充满冰块的机器，
run through a bypass machine filled with ice,

60
00:04:33,700 --> 00:04:37,820
接着泵回他的静脉和动脉。
then pumped back through his veins and arteries.

61
00:04:37,940 --> 00:04:42,260
这样就逐渐的把他的体温降到18摄氏度--
This has gradually cooled him down to 18 degrees Centigrade --

62
00:04:42,370 --> 00:04:45,930
就是55华氏度。
55 Fahrenheit.

63
00:04:46,040 --> 00:04:51,450
这样他的细胞和神经的活动就很微弱了。
The activity of his cells and neurons cannot be measured.

64
00:04:53,650 --> 00:04:56,610
如果你让一个普通医生，
If you had a general practitioner

65
00:04:56,720 --> 00:04:59,680
或者心脏病学家或其他什么人进来，
or a cardiologist or somebody come in

66
00:04:59,790 --> 00:05:03,520
使用他们常规标准判断生死，
and use their regular criteria for life or death,

67
00:05:03,630 --> 00:05:05,920
这位病人就满足所有的死亡条件。
all the criteria for death would be fulfilled.

68
00:05:06,030 --> 00:05:10,160
这个时候，心肺机和呼吸机--
At this point, the heart-lung machine and the respirator --

69
00:05:10,270 --> 00:05:14,190
所有保持他活着的仪器--都关闭了--
the devices that keep him alive -- are shut off --

70
00:05:14,310 --> 00:05:17,530
没有呼吸，不会泵血--
no breathing, no blood pumping --

71
00:05:17,640 --> 00:05:22,280
就是一个等同于死亡的情况。
a condition virtually identical to death.

72
00:05:25,320 --> 00:05:29,210
John有45分钟的时间可以安全手术，
John has 45 minutes to operate in safety.

73
00:05:29,320 --> 00:05:32,710
一个小时后，开始出现脑损伤。
After an hour, brain damage will set in.

74
00:05:32,820 --> 00:05:36,450
现在，他几十年的经验开始发挥作用。
Now his decades of experience come into play.

75
00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:39,860
我们仍处于病人的假死状态，
We're still in suspended animation.

76
00:05:42,870 --> 00:05:46,040
我还还有15分钟的安全手术时间。
So we've got 15 minutes to go for safety.

77
00:05:47,870 --> 00:05:49,430
最后，
Finally,

78
00:05:49,540 --> 00:05:52,200
John和他的团队在还剩7分钟的时候
John and his team manage to complete the repairs

79
00:05:52,310 --> 00:05:54,770
完成了修复。
with seven minutes to spare.

80
00:05:54,880 --> 00:05:59,200
这个病人事实上已经死亡了38分钟，
The patient has been virtually dead for 38 minutes.

81
00:05:59,320 --> 00:06:02,210
他们慢慢地把生命维持装置打开，
They slowly bring back life support,

82
00:06:02,320 --> 00:06:04,310
让他脑部没有损伤地
returning him to the land of the living with

83
00:06:04,420 --> 00:06:07,060
回到这个世界。
no damage to the brain.

84
00:06:10,630 --> 00:06:14,620
每天我都会对做到这种程度的手术感到惊奇，
Each and every day, I'm amazed that this can be done.

85
00:06:14,730 --> 00:06:18,160
死亡的定义已经改变了，
The definition of death has changed, really,

86
00:06:18,270 --> 00:06:20,590
只有永久性的死亡才算死亡。
and death is death when it's permanent.

87
00:06:20,710 --> 00:06:21,890
但这些标准
But those criteria

88
00:06:22,010 --> 00:06:26,270
对于这个非常非常特殊的
in this very, very special high-technology scenario

89
00:06:26,380 --> 00:06:29,430
假死或低温心搏停止的高科技环境--
of suspended animation or deep hypothermic arrest --

90
00:06:29,550 --> 00:06:32,270
死亡的标准并不适用。
those criteria for death don't really apply.

91
00:06:32,380 --> 00:06:35,240
但是我们能否复活那些
But can we bring life back

92
00:06:35,350 --> 00:06:38,990
在更少可控情况下死去的人呢？
to those who die in less controlled situations?

93
00:06:45,130 --> 00:06:48,220
Lance Becker是宾夕法尼亚大学
Lance Becker is the director of the University of Pennsylvania's

94
00:06:48,330 --> 00:06:51,260
复苏科学中心的主管，
Center for Resuscitation Science.

95
00:06:51,370 --> 00:06:54,390
他认为让死者复活的关键
He believes the key to resurrection

96
00:06:54,510 --> 00:06:57,060
就埋藏在我们的细胞中。
is buried deep within our cells.

97
00:06:57,180 --> 00:07:01,070
有个原理我们都已经知道了几千年了，
Well, what we've known for just literally thousands of years

98
00:07:01,180 --> 00:07:06,340
那就是你把肉冷藏，就能更长时间的保鲜。
is that, if you keep meat cold, it keeps longer.

99
00:07:06,450 --> 00:07:10,510
腐烂的过程其实也是死亡的过程--
The decay process, which is actually that death process --

100
00:07:10,620 --> 00:07:15,380
所有的东西在低温下
All of those things are slowed down

101
00:07:15,490 --> 00:07:17,450
都能减缓。
in the cold setting.

102
00:07:17,560 --> 00:07:19,120
当温度下降，
When the temperature comes down,

103
00:07:19,230 --> 00:07:21,150
细胞就不需要很多氧气，
the cells don't need as much oxygen,

104
00:07:21,270 --> 00:07:24,620
它们的代谢程度变弱，
they don't metabolize as much,

105
00:07:24,740 --> 00:07:30,840
基本进入了一种慢动作、冬眠的状态，
and they essentially sort of go into slow-mo, hibernation-style.

106
00:07:32,540 --> 00:07:37,970
你的身体由数十万亿个活细胞组成，
Your body is made of tens of trillions of living cells.

107
00:07:38,080 --> 00:07:42,170
相关的基因告诉细胞做什么，
Regulatory genes tell these cells how to behave.

108
00:07:42,290 --> 00:07:46,180
它们算是细胞的操作系统，
Think of them as the cells' operating system.

109
00:07:46,290 --> 00:07:49,550
在细胞生命的最后时期，
At the end of a cell's life, the genes produce

110
00:07:49,660 --> 00:07:53,020
基因会产生一些破坏细胞的酶将细胞降解，
destructive enzymes that tear the cell apart.

111
00:07:53,130 --> 00:07:58,970
一个人每天大约有五百亿个细胞死亡。
Every day, roughly 50 billion of your cells die.

112
00:08:01,040 --> 00:08:03,430
当有些情况很糟糕时--
When something goes very wrong --

113
00:08:03,540 --> 00:08:06,930
比如你心脏骤停--
say, you suffer a cardiac arrest --

114
00:08:07,050 --> 00:08:10,440
受损的细胞就会发出警报，
the injured cells sound an alarm,

115
00:08:10,550 --> 00:08:15,370
告诉它周围健康的细胞是时候凋亡了。
telling their healthy neighbors it's time to die.

116
00:08:15,490 --> 00:08:18,310
这样发出的一波让细胞自杀的信号
This sets off a wave of cellular suicide

117
00:08:18,420 --> 00:08:21,260
快速传遍全身。
that spreads rapidly across the body.

118
00:08:22,330 --> 00:08:25,750
我们不会偶然死亡，
We don't die by accident.

119
00:08:25,860 --> 00:08:31,190
而是一种生物程序
There's biological programming

120
00:08:31,300 --> 00:08:35,490
控制着死亡。
that actually controls the way we die.

121
00:08:35,610 --> 00:08:37,900
在这个程序中，
And in that programming

122
00:08:38,010 --> 00:08:42,200
我们就有机会对其加以修饰，
is the opportunity for modifying that program

123
00:08:42,310 --> 00:08:45,170
这样就能改变结果--
so that we can alter the outcome --

124
00:08:45,280 --> 00:08:46,870
把某些人从死亡中复活。
bring someone back to life.

125
00:08:46,990 --> 00:08:50,740
为了寻找什么引发了死亡程序，
To find out what triggers the death program,

126
00:08:50,860 --> 00:08:55,250
Lance取正常细胞并让其缺氧，
Lance took healthy cells and starved them of oxygen.

127
00:08:55,360 --> 00:08:59,180
他希望看到大多数细胞都死掉，
He expected most of the cells to die

128
00:08:59,300 --> 00:09:04,420
而存活下来的在恢复氧气供应后继续生存。
and the survivors to flourish when oxygen was restored.

129
00:09:04,540 --> 00:09:05,820
那么我发现的
So, what I found

130
00:09:05,940 --> 00:09:09,460
和我们本来期望的正好相反，
was the opposite of what we thought we would find,

131
00:09:09,570 --> 00:09:11,730
当细胞缺氧时，
which is the cells without oxygen

132
00:09:11,840 --> 00:09:14,330
它们就只是停在那儿。
just sort of laid there.

133
00:09:14,450 --> 00:09:17,970
它们什么都不做，也不会死亡。
They didn't do anything, but they didn't die.

134
00:09:18,080 --> 00:09:22,110
但当氧气恢复后，
But what happened was, when we reoxygenated those cells,

135
00:09:22,220 --> 00:09:24,980
细胞就开始死亡。
that's when cell death occurred.

136
00:09:25,090 --> 00:09:27,210
所以有点讽刺的是
So it is a little bit ironic

137
00:09:27,330 --> 00:09:32,320
氧气，是我们喜爱而且生存必须依赖的分子，
that oxygen, the molecule that we love and that we live with,

138
00:09:32,430 --> 00:09:36,560
却成了推动细胞死亡的分子。
becomes the molecule that drives death.

139
00:09:36,670 --> 00:09:41,430
不知怎么，重新将氧气分子引入细胞
Somehow, reintroducing oxygen into cells

140
00:09:41,540 --> 00:09:43,760
会引起细胞死亡信号，
triggers the death signal

141
00:09:43,880 --> 00:09:46,980
让它们大量的死去。
that causes them to commit mass suicide.

142
00:09:48,180 --> 00:09:51,740
但低温似乎可以阻断这一过程。
Freezing seems to interrupt this process.

143
00:09:51,850 --> 00:09:53,570
Lance加紧工作，
Lance pressed on,

144
00:09:53,690 --> 00:09:56,880
因为他知道如果他发现了死亡信号的源头，
knowing that, if he found the death signal's source,

145
00:09:56,990 --> 00:09:59,780
也许就能无需低温
he might be able to stop it from transmitting

146
00:09:59,890 --> 00:10:02,650
就可以阻断死亡信号。
without the need for freezing.

147
00:10:02,760 --> 00:10:04,680
我们开始问我们自己，
As we began to ask ourselves,

148
00:10:04,800 --> 00:10:08,250
“该如何解释细胞这类奇怪的行为？”
"What could explain this sort of bizarre behavior?"

149
00:10:08,370 --> 00:10:11,560
我们开始从细胞信号通路着手，
We started seeing cellular pathways.

150
00:10:11,670 --> 00:10:16,500
所有的通路都把我们引向细胞内的一种细胞器，
All of the paths led us to the organelle inside our cells

151
00:10:16,610 --> 00:10:19,210
我们称之为线粒体。
that we call the mitochondria.

152
00:10:20,880 --> 00:10:26,300
线粒体存在于你身体的每个细胞中，
Mitochondria lie deep within every single cell of your body.

153
00:10:26,420 --> 00:10:29,840
它们消耗你吃的食物和吸入的氧气，
They take the food you eat and the oxygen you breathe

154
00:10:29,950 --> 00:10:33,310
并把它们转化成化学能，
and convert them into chemical energy.

155
00:10:33,420 --> 00:10:38,950
某种方式来看的话，这有点像核电站，
In some ways, it's a little bit like a nuclear power plant.

156
00:10:39,060 --> 00:10:41,390
你知道，在核电站内，
And you know how, in a nuclear power plant,

157
00:10:41,500 --> 00:10:44,820
有许多控制棒控制反应堆的速率来产生热量，
there are rods that come together and they produce the heat,

158
00:10:44,940 --> 00:10:46,560
如果你不去控制反应速率，
and if you don't control that process,

159
00:10:46,670 --> 00:10:49,230
就会以切尔诺贝利终结。
you end up with Chernobyl.

160
00:10:49,340 --> 00:10:54,070
实际发生的是当一个线粒体信号传到细胞核，
What happens is, after one mitochondria goes nuclear,

161
00:10:54,180 --> 00:10:57,240
就起始了一个链式反应，
it starts to trigger a chain reaction

162
00:10:57,350 --> 00:10:59,740
放大了死亡信号，
that amplifies the death signal,

163
00:10:59,850 --> 00:11:04,090
死亡信号就会传遍全身。
and that death signal can be spread throughout the body.

164
00:11:05,160 --> 00:11:08,180
Lance和他的团队认为
Lance and his team suspect

165
00:11:08,290 --> 00:11:11,980
我们能够通过使用硫化物、氰化物和一氧化碳
we might be able to stop this chain reaction

166
00:11:12,100 --> 00:11:14,420
来使我们的线粒体中毒，
by poisoning our mitochondria

167
00:11:14,530 --> 00:11:18,760
从而停止死亡的链式反应。
with sulfide, cyanide, and carbon monoxide.

168
00:11:18,870 --> 00:11:21,790
使用准确校准的毒性物质的剂量，
A finely calibrated dose of these toxins

169
00:11:21,910 --> 00:11:23,560
有可能中断死亡信号。
might disarm the death signal.

170
00:11:23,680 --> 00:11:25,700
这会比低温起效更快，
It will be quicker than freezing,

171
00:11:25,810 --> 00:11:29,780
来防止细胞崩溃。
and it could reverse a cellular meltdown.

172
00:11:32,450 --> 00:11:34,740
那么理想情况下，我们想要做的是
So, ideally, what we'd like to do

173
00:11:34,850 --> 00:11:38,410
在我们给病人恢复通氧时，
is we'd like to get a few of those molecules on board

174
00:11:38,520 --> 00:11:41,680
使用一点这些毒性分子。
as we're beginning to bring oxygen back to the patient.

175
00:11:41,790 --> 00:11:44,880
我们认为我们能重启线粒体，
We think we can restart that mitochondria,

176
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:48,950
也将其恢复来产生能量，
have it convert back to producing energy

177
00:11:49,070 --> 00:11:52,790
而不会引发死亡。
instead of producing death.

178
00:11:52,900 --> 00:11:56,060
这些处理手段仍处于试验阶段，
These treatments are still highly experimental.

179
00:11:56,170 --> 00:11:59,530
但如果医生能够沉默死亡信号，
But if doctors can silence the death signal,

180
00:11:59,640 --> 00:12:01,700
将正在或最近死去的人复活
it may soon be commonplace

181
00:12:01,810 --> 00:12:05,350
就会变得非常普遍了。
to revive the dying and the recently dead.

182
00:12:06,380 --> 00:12:08,510
医学科学已经证明
Medical science has already proven

183
00:12:08,620 --> 00:12:10,240
在严格控制多种因素的情况下，
that the dead can live again

184
00:12:10,360 --> 00:12:14,150
可以将死者复活。
under a very controlled set of circumstances.

185
00:12:14,260 --> 00:12:16,530
但我们能更进一步么？
But can it take us further?

186
00:12:17,730 --> 00:12:20,830
如果我们能重新将死者培育出来呢？
What if we could grow the dead back to life?

187
00:12:23,740 --> 00:12:26,410
想象下如果把爱因斯坦或莫扎特从坟墓中复活，
Imagine how much richer humanity might be

188
00:12:27,400 --> 00:12:31,330
人类的文化会多么富饶，
if we could raise Einstein or Mozart from the grave

189
00:12:31,440 --> 00:12:34,730
或是把我们逝去的深爱的人带回来，
or how much it would mean to us personally

190
00:12:34,840 --> 00:12:39,110
对于个人又是多么意义重大。
if we could bring back the loved ones we have lost?

191
00:12:41,380 --> 00:12:43,840
这都是有可能的。
It may be possible.

192
00:12:43,950 --> 00:12:48,540
克隆技术带给我们了一条通往复活的新道路...
Cloning has opened up a new road to resurrection...

193
00:12:48,660 --> 00:12:51,060
但我们应该这么做么？
But should we take it?

194
00:12:52,530 --> 00:12:57,150
Bob Lanza出生于波士顿的一个工薪阶层家庭，
Bob Lanza was born into a working-class Boston family.

195
00:12:57,270 --> 00:13:00,120
今天，他在马萨诸塞州拥有了自己的小岛，
Today, he owns an island in Massachusetts

196
00:13:00,240 --> 00:13:02,590
生活在一幢建筑物中，
and lives in a sprawling compound

197
00:13:02,700 --> 00:13:06,560
这里一部分是家，一部分是自然历史博物馆。
that's part house, part natural-history museum.

198
00:13:06,680 --> 00:13:11,630
这些都是他在生物技术职业中取得的成果。
These are the fruits of a career in biotechnology.

199
00:13:11,750 --> 00:13:14,070
这是一个雷龙的股骨，
Here's a brontosaurus femur.

200
00:13:14,180 --> 00:13:16,310
人们走进屋子看到它的第一个问题就是，
One of the first things people ask me when they come into the house

201
00:13:16,420 --> 00:13:18,070
“Bob，你会去克隆它么？”
is, "Bob, are you gonna clone that?"

202
00:13:18,190 --> 00:13:20,180
然后我告诉他们，“你无法从石头中克隆，
And I tell them, "you can't clone from stone.

203
00:13:20,290 --> 00:13:21,310
克隆需要一个活细胞。“
You need a living cell."

204
00:13:21,420 --> 00:13:24,950
过去十年，
Over the last decade,

205
00:13:25,060 --> 00:13:30,090
Bob成功克隆了鼠、牛、猫、狗、
Bob has successfully cloned mice, cows, cats, dogs,

206
00:13:30,200 --> 00:13:33,760
猪、羊和 马。
pigs, sheep, and horses.

207
00:13:33,870 --> 00:13:38,090
2001年，他使用冻结的细胞和
In 2001, he used frozen cells to successfully resurrect

208
00:13:38,210 --> 00:13:43,170
作为代孕母亲的美国牛，成功复活了
an extinct southeast Asian ox called a Gaur,

209
00:13:43,280 --> 00:13:47,400
名为白肢野牛已灭绝的东南亚牛。
using an American cow as a surrogate mother.

210
00:13:47,520 --> 00:13:48,940
每个人都说，“不，这不会成功的，
Everyone said, "no, that won't work.

211
00:13:49,050 --> 00:13:51,270
你无法使用另外一个物种的卵细胞克隆这个物种。”
You can't clone one species using the egg from another one."

212
00:13:51,390 --> 00:13:53,440
然后我说，“不不不，如果它们的种属亲缘关系够近，
And I said, "no, no, no. If we do it and it's close enough,

213
00:13:53,560 --> 00:13:54,640
我们能够做成的。”
we should get it to work."

214
00:13:54,760 --> 00:13:56,750
然后我取了白肢野牛的皮肤细胞，
So I actually took skin cells from the Gaur,

215
00:13:56,860 --> 00:13:59,650
把其中的DNA放在了一个普通牛的卵细胞中，
and I actually put the DNA into an ordinary cow egg.

216
00:13:59,760 --> 00:14:02,820
然后我们就创造出了这个惊人的小小的白肢野牛胚胎，
And then we created these spectacular little Gaur embryos,

217
00:14:02,930 --> 00:14:04,890
然后我们把它们运到衣阿华州，
and we shipped them off to Iowa,

218
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,220
把这些胚胎移植到普通牛体内。
where they were implanted into an ordinary cow.

219
00:14:07,340 --> 00:14:08,990
10个月之后，
And it turns out that, 10 months later,

220
00:14:09,100 --> 00:14:12,230
这只漂亮的白肢野牛就出生了。
we had this beautiful little baby Gaur that was born.

221
00:14:12,340 --> 00:14:14,660
它看起来就像一只小驯鹿--非常可爱。
It looked like a little baby reindeer -- just adorable.

222
00:14:14,780 --> 00:14:20,200
但Bob看起来很漂亮，有些人认为它是怪胎。
But where Bob sees beauty, others see monstrosity.

223
00:14:20,320 --> 00:14:24,010
克隆技术的反对者称他是在扮演上帝，
Opponents of cloning say he's playing God.

224
00:14:24,120 --> 00:14:26,940
但Bob并不这么看。
Bob doesn't see it that way.

225
00:14:27,060 --> 00:14:29,510
克隆其实并不是那么不寻常，
Cloning really isn't all that unusual.

226
00:14:29,620 --> 00:14:32,650
几千年来，人们一直都在克隆植物，
For thousands of years, people have been cloning plants.

227
00:14:32,760 --> 00:14:35,950
你完全可以减掉植物的一部分--
You can actually start by taking a clipping from a plant --

228
00:14:36,060 --> 00:14:38,720
这样就得到了一些遗传物质--
you get some of the genetic material --

229
00:14:38,830 --> 00:14:42,500
然后加点营养物...
and then add a little nutrients...

230
00:14:44,710 --> 00:14:45,830
让它生根，
...get it to root,

231
00:14:45,940 --> 00:14:49,540
基本上这样就能克隆出一株完整的生物体。
and to basically clone an entire new organism.

232
00:14:50,880 --> 00:14:54,270
人类已经掌握了植物的克隆技术，
Humanity has mastered the cloning of plants,

233
00:14:54,380 --> 00:14:57,910
现在我们正前进到动物王国。
and we are now working our way through the animal kingdom.

234
00:14:58,020 --> 00:15:01,080
是否会有那么一天，我们从死者身上取点DNA，
Could we someday take the DNA of dead people

235
00:15:01,190 --> 00:15:03,250
然后克隆复活他们？
and bring their bodies back to life?

236
00:15:03,360 --> 00:15:07,260
我们能否耕种一个复活的人类的花园？
Can we cultivate a garden of resurrected humans?

237
00:15:10,070 --> 00:15:11,950
想象这样的一个世界，
Imagine a world

238
00:15:12,070 --> 00:15:14,790
一个女人可以通过怀上自己丈夫的遗传物质，
where a woman could bring back her deceased husband

239
00:15:14,900 --> 00:15:17,130
来复活已经死去的他...
by giving birth to him...

240
00:15:17,240 --> 00:15:19,300
或是一个男人可以复活他的母亲，
or a man could bring back his mother

241
00:15:19,410 --> 00:15:22,100
然后把她当成女儿养育。
and raise her as his daughter.

242
00:15:22,210 --> 00:15:25,100
如果我们拥有死者的活细胞，
If we have a living cell of a dead person,

243
00:15:25,210 --> 00:15:28,470
当然，我们理论上是可以克隆出死者的个体的。
certainly, we could, in theory, clone that individual.

244
00:15:28,580 --> 00:15:31,310
我们在一两年前发表了一篇论文，
We published a paper about a year or two ago

245
00:15:31,420 --> 00:15:34,680
其中表明我们确实可以创造人类胚胎，
where we show we can actually now create human embryos

246
00:15:34,790 --> 00:15:38,410
和正常人类胚胎是一样的。
that are genetically identical to a normal embryo.

247
00:15:38,530 --> 00:15:40,420
你应该知道的唯一的
The only way you would really know

248
00:15:40,530 --> 00:15:42,550
能否克隆一个人的方法，
whether you could clone a human being

249
00:15:42,660 --> 00:15:44,550
就是需要把克隆出来的胚胎
is to actually implant that embryo

250
00:15:44,670 --> 00:15:46,720
植入代孕母亲的子宫里。
into the uterus of a surrogate mother.

251
00:15:46,840 --> 00:15:48,190
当然，我们不能植入那些--
Of course, we cannot implant those --

252
00:15:48,300 --> 00:15:49,630
被认为不符合伦理的胚胎--
that would be considered unethical --

253
00:15:49,740 --> 00:15:51,290
因为还不清楚他们是否能够--
so it's unclear whether or not they would --

254
00:15:51,410 --> 00:15:52,640
成长为人类。
would give rise to a human being.

255
00:15:53,970 --> 00:15:59,840
社会中人们强烈反对克隆人。
Society has vehemently rejected reproductive human cloning.

256
00:15:59,950 --> 00:16:03,040
在这样的环境下，遗传学家
In this climate, it is extremely difficult

257
00:16:03,150 --> 00:16:07,580
极难获得研究经费。
for geneticists to obtain funding for their research.

258
00:16:07,690 --> 00:16:10,450
另外克隆材料也是很缺乏的，
There is also a shortage of material.

259
00:16:10,560 --> 00:16:12,770
克隆人的一个问题就是
So, one of the problems with human cloning

260
00:16:12,890 --> 00:16:13,890
卵细胞的供应。
is the supply of eggs.

261
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:15,280
如果克隆老鼠或者牛，
So, with a mouse or a cow,

262
00:16:15,400 --> 00:16:18,120
就算不是上千个，
我们也可以毫不夸张地得到数百个卵细胞。
we can get literally hundreds, if not thousands, of eggs.

263
00:16:18,230 --> 00:16:20,120
举个例子，我们到屠宰场，
We can go to the slaughterhouse, for instance,

264
00:16:20,240 --> 00:16:21,420
每个卵细胞一美元，
and get them for a dollar each

265
00:16:21,540 --> 00:16:23,060
然后就能得到上千个牛卵细胞。
and get thousands of these cow eggs.

266
00:16:23,170 --> 00:16:27,030
而人类，就要花一年多才能得到5个卵细胞，
With the humans, it took us over a year just to get five eggs.

267
00:16:27,140 --> 00:16:29,330
即使你有了卵细胞，
Even if you had the eggs,

268
00:16:29,440 --> 00:16:31,730
还需要上百次的怀孕试验
it could take hundreds of pregnancies

269
00:16:31,850 --> 00:16:34,300
才能完美克隆出人类，
to perfect human cloning,

270
00:16:34,420 --> 00:16:35,740
这个过程中会产生
a process that could result

271
00:16:35,850 --> 00:16:40,140
大量的带有可怕的遗传损伤的婴儿。
in scores of babies with horrific genetic damage.

272
00:16:40,260 --> 00:16:41,440
这就很像--
It'd be very much like --

273
00:16:41,560 --> 00:16:43,450
如果你想克隆你的孩子，
if you wanted to clone your child,

274
00:16:43,560 --> 00:16:45,480
就像把他送上火箭，
it'd be like sending him up in a rocket

275
00:16:45,590 --> 00:16:47,380
而有一半的几率火箭会爆炸。
with a 50-50 chance it would blow up.

276
00:16:47,500 --> 00:16:52,690
几乎全世界，克隆人类都是违法的。
In most of the world, cloning humans is not illegal.

277
00:16:52,800 --> 00:16:57,960
也许有一天，某个游手好闲的科学家会成功克隆出人类。
One day, a rogue scientist will pull it off.

278
00:16:58,070 --> 00:17:04,610
但克隆某个人和复制一个人是不同的。
But cloning a person is not the same as duplicating a person.

279
00:17:07,180 --> 00:17:09,640
许多人都养宠物--
A lot of people, you know, who have their pets --

280
00:17:09,750 --> 00:17:12,240
他们通常希望克隆它们，重新得到自己的毛绒宠物。
they want to often clone them, and they want Fluffy back.

281
00:17:12,350 --> 00:17:14,780
而我告诉他们，“你们是无法得到你们的原来的宠物的。”
And what I tell them is, "you're not gonna get Fluffy back."

282
00:17:14,890 --> 00:17:17,850
原因是，我们虽然从同一只动物身上的
As a matter of fact, we actually clone entire herds of cows

283
00:17:17,960 --> 00:17:20,080
单个细胞克隆出了一大群牛，
from a single cell from the same animal,

284
00:17:20,200 --> 00:17:22,020
它们已经发展成了完整的层级群体，
and they develop a whole hierarchy,

285
00:17:22,130 --> 00:17:23,420
就像人类一样。
just like we do in humans.

286
00:17:23,530 --> 00:17:25,720
你得到的有胆小的牛，也有攻击性很强的牛，
So you have timid cows and aggressive cows,

287
00:17:25,830 --> 00:17:26,960
但它们全是一只牛的克隆。
and they're all clones.

288
00:17:27,070 --> 00:17:29,290
它们发展出了自己的行为模式，
So they develop their own behavioral patters.

289
00:17:29,400 --> 00:17:30,530
所以环境
So the environment

290
00:17:30,640 --> 00:17:34,130
对于一个人的发展有着深远的影响。
has a very profound impact on your development.

291
00:17:34,240 --> 00:17:39,170
假如说我们从爱因斯坦的头发中获得了DNA，
Let's say we decide to take DNA from Einstein's hair

292
00:17:39,280 --> 00:17:42,240
克隆出了一些新爱因斯坦，
and grow some new Einsteins.

293
00:17:42,350 --> 00:17:47,540
这些克隆体可能并不是那个能写出“E=MC平方”的人，
Those clones would not be the man who wrote "E = MC squared."

294
00:17:47,660 --> 00:17:50,010
他们每人都会有被环境塑造的
Each would have a unique personality

295
00:17:50,130 --> 00:17:52,360
自己的个性。
shaped by his environment.

296
00:17:53,830 --> 00:17:59,220
克隆人就像相隔数年才出生的双胞胎--
Clones are like identical twins born years apart --

297
00:17:59,330 --> 00:18:05,160
他们很相似，但又不会一样。
they may be similar, but they will not be the same.

298
00:18:05,270 --> 00:18:07,900
也许从生到死的关键
Perhaps the key to life after death

299
00:18:08,010 --> 00:18:10,630
并不在得到一个完整的新躯体，
is not to grow an entirely new body,

300
00:18:10,750 --> 00:18:12,850
而是确确实实复活那个人。
but to resurrect the one you have.

301
00:18:15,820 --> 00:18:20,390
目前，我们的社会不允许克隆人类，
At the moment, our society does not permit human cloning,

302
00:18:21,100 --> 00:18:22,960
但这是会变的。
but that could change.

303
00:18:23,160 --> 00:18:24,780
与此同时，
In the meantime,

304
00:18:24,900 --> 00:18:29,020
生物技术专家有另一个锦囊妙计--
biotechnologists have another trick up their sleeves --

305
00:18:29,130 --> 00:18:32,860
一块一块地复活人类。
resurrecting people piece by piece.

306
00:18:32,970 --> 00:18:36,930
这是种方法可改变医学科学
It's an approach that could transform medical science

307
00:18:37,040 --> 00:18:41,830
并模糊生死的界限。
and blur the line between life and death.

308
00:18:41,950 --> 00:18:44,970
这项革命始于这里，
The revolution begins here,

309
00:18:45,080 --> 00:18:48,140
明尼苏达大学的实验室，
in a laboratory the University of Minnesota.

310
00:18:48,250 --> 00:18:51,510
这里也是Dr. Doris Taylor
This is where Dr. Doris Taylor

311
00:18:51,620 --> 00:18:54,710
将死者复活的地方。
breathes life back into the dead.

312
00:18:54,830 --> 00:18:57,080
我只是希望能够改变世界，
I just want to change the world.

313
00:18:57,190 --> 00:18:59,780
为那些遭受疾病的人。
I want to change the world for people with disease.

314
00:18:59,900 --> 00:19:02,620
我有个兄弟也得了慢性病，
I also have a brother who's chronically ill,

315
00:19:02,730 --> 00:19:06,490
或许这也影响了我一生中做的每件事。
and that's probably influenced everything I've done in my life.

316
00:19:06,600 --> 00:19:10,490
Doris正在通过培育死者的部分身体
Doris is changing the world by growing new body parts

317
00:19:10,610 --> 00:19:12,760
改变世界。
from the shells of old ones.

318
00:19:12,880 --> 00:19:16,830
她从尸体上取得器官，
She takes organs from cadavers and reanimates them

319
00:19:16,950 --> 00:19:22,550
用一个不太可能的的方法复活它们--建筑学。
using a method drawn from an unlikely source -- architecture.

320
00:19:28,190 --> 00:19:29,750
构成一个建筑物的砖块，
The bricks in a building

321
00:19:29,860 --> 00:19:32,250
就像一个器官中的细胞--
are just like the cells in an organ --

322
00:19:32,360 --> 00:19:34,520
种类不同、形状不同，
different kinds, different shapes.

323
00:19:34,630 --> 00:19:36,020
它们构成房间，
And they make rooms,

324
00:19:36,130 --> 00:19:39,390
这些房间就像心脏的腔室，
and the rooms are like the chambers of a heart.

325
00:19:39,500 --> 00:19:42,890
它们经门口相连，比如瓣膜，
They're connected by doorways, like the valves.

326
00:19:43,010 --> 00:19:45,600
它们也有高速公路，比如动脉和静脉。
They have hallways, like the arteries and veins.

327
00:19:45,710 --> 00:19:48,600
基本上，你可以把一个器官
Essentially, you can think of an organ

328
00:19:48,710 --> 00:19:51,210
当做一栋建筑物，
just like you think of a building.

329
00:19:52,150 --> 00:19:56,270
可以通过砖块重新建成建筑物。
Just as buildings can be rebuilt brick by brick,

330
00:19:56,390 --> 00:20:00,980
